## Key architectural decisions

- Two parallel AI calls (summary, calendar), not one. The wheel renders immediately from the chart JSON with no AI wait; the summary fills in on the chart page; the calendar generates in the background while the person reads and enters their email. The download waits for it with an intentional state ("Still writing your year…").
- The chart JSON is the single source of truth for natal data. Wheel, summary call, transit engine and calendar call all read from it; nothing recomputes or re-enters natal degrees. It stores raw ecliptic longitudes, not only signs and houses.
- Chart data flows: form → calculation → chart JSON → (wheel) + (summary call) + (transit engine → calendar call → ICS)
- Unknown birth time defaults to a solar chart (planets at noon, Sun's sign as house 1) — handle gracefully in UI and skip house-based detail entirely: transits carry `house: null`, prompts don't name life areas, and the wheel shows planets on a plain ring without house dividers
- One naming scheme everywhere: `PlanetId` (sun…pluto), `PointId` = `PlanetId | 'asc' | 'mc'`, `AspectId`. The chart JSON, transit engine, summary `pointIds` and wheel all use these IDs. The chart JSON includes natal aspects.
- Transit filtering: outer planets (Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, Pluto) to personal points (Sun, Moon, Mercury, Venus, Mars, Ascendant) only. Major aspects only (conjunction, opposition, square). Window: twelve months from download. Keep the window and planet list as parameters, not constants.
- Exact hits are found by solving for the moment the separation crosses the aspect angle, not by daily sampling. Retrograde loops produce multiple hits of the same transit: each is its own entry, tagged with `pass` and `totalPasses`.
- The engine also outputs, per month, the transits in orb and any outer planet stations; these feed the monthly cards.
- With unknown birth time, exclude the Ascendant (it sits on the Sun) AND the Moon (a noon Moon can be ~6.5° off, shifting slow-transit dates by weeks or months) from dated transit targets
- Pattern cards carry `pointIds`; the wheel emphasises those points and shows matching markers. No astrological glyphs in user-facing wheel labels.
- Email capture is a simple field before download — no account creation

## What NOT to build

Do not add or suggest any of these — they are out of MVP scope:
- User accounts or authentication
- Database or persistence beyond the session
- Payment or premium tiers
- Paid documents: natal reading, multi-year timing guide, decision filter (see `docs/roadmap-v2.md`)
- Any Vedic / sidereal calculation, nakshatras or dasha periods
- Famous person chart matches
- Interactive chart explorer
- Google Calendar API integration (ICS download only)
- Content automation or social posting
- Mobile app
- Dating/synastry features

## Error handling

- AI API failures (including failed zod validation): retry once, then show a graceful fallback message
- If the calendar call fails, the chart page stays intact (wheel + summary); the download area offers a retry
- Geocoder failures or no results: let the user retry or rephrase; never block on it silently
- Loading states during AI generation feel intentional ("Reading your chart...", "Still writing your year…"), not broken
- No server-side cache (serverless memory isn't reliable and there's no database). Store both AI results in `sessionStorage`, keyed by a hash of birth data + context answers, wrapped in try/catch, so a refresh restores them instead of calling the API again

## When in doubt

- Simpler is better — this ships in 2–3 weeks
- If it's not in the MVP column of the spec, it doesn't get built
- The questionnaire feels calm and paced, not like a data entry form
- Every piece of user-facing text sounds like the brand voice
- Ask before adding any dependency not listed above
